00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.792
throughout, I think it's
good to have a discussion.

00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:06.000
We're each going to be
talking about different areas.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.040
So feel free to put your hands
up or come up to the microphone

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:09.730
here.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.440
The presenters have
told me they prefer

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.100
that so that they can actually
hear you on the recording.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.477
But feel free to
ask questions or let

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.810
us know if there's things you
want us to clarify when it

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.000
comes IoT connectivity options.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.910
So if you've been to
the other IoT sessions

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.820
at NEXT the previous two
days, you've probably

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:34.330
seen this slide a lot, but
we're connecting more things

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.102
to the internet
than ever before.

00:01:36.102 --> 00:01:37.810
But actually, what
this session is about,

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.435
is about those things
that haven't been

00:01:39.435 --> 00:01:41.440
connected to the internet yet.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.230
For one reason or another,
they struggle with the existing

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.330
connectivity options.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.890
And there's generally
a few reasons

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.170
that products or
companies struggle

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.180
when they're trying to connect
things to the internet.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.420
And it comes down to
power, cost, and range,

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.970
or some combination
of the three.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.920
So first of all, power--

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.230
there are a lot of devices
that don't have main power

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.630
or can't be
recharged frequently.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.700
And that makes it
hard, in some cases,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.520
to actually get the connectivity
to them that they need,

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.420
especially if they
need to broadcast

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.750
long ranges over wireless.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.470
Next is cost-- and
this comes down

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:21.070
to module cost, as well
as recurring costs that it

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:24.700
takes to actually connect
devices to the internet.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.650
And this is especially a problem
for devices or applications

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.860
where you need to connect
a huge number of devices

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:37.090
in order to get significant ROI
on whatever your use case is.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.580
Even as module costs come
down and connectivity costs

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.907
come down, there
are still use cases

00:02:41.907 --> 00:02:43.990
that need it to be cheaper,
need it to be smaller,

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.920
need it to be more
power efficient.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.400
And, finally, range,
which generally comes into

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.210
account on
compounding these two.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.232
So we have pretty good
low power options today.

00:02:55.232 --> 00:02:57.190
We have pretty good
connectivity infrastructure

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.300
that you can connect
to for pretty cheap.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.220
But if you need your devices to
connect over very long ranges,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.010
or in lots of
situations, then this

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:10.160
can also be a big challenge.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.280
So let's talk about
some of the use cases

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.440
that this is a challenge for.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.270
One that I think is
very interesting,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:20.270
and is applicable to LPWAN, is
metering and smart metering.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:23.110
So this has been a problem
that smart cities and energy

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.900
companies have wanted to solve
for a long time, where instead

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.320
of sending somebody around to
each energy meter to read it,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.410
they want it to be able
to report automatically

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:33.040
all the time.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.950
But there are challenges here
because the energy company

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.570
typically doesn't control
the connectivity, which

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:42.070
is near the meter.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:45.410
Generally, energy meters are in
weird locations in buildings.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.890
And, honestly, it's pretty
cheap to send somebody

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.720
around and read each
meter individually.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.420
So in order to get
good ROI, reading them

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.070
has to be radically cheaper.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:56.660
Next is agriculture.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.180
So, agriculture, whether
its livestock or farming,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.310
has challenges in
connectivity because you

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.710
have big open spaces,
and in some cases,

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.060
you have lots of assets.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.540
So you can't put a cell
phone on every single cow

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.360
that you have to
track its location.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.550
And if you have
large open fields,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.829
you might need lots of
environmental sensors

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.769
in areas where there isn't even
good cellular connectivity,

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:24.160
let alone mains power
to keep them going.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.530
And, finally, one that Vic
is going to talk to you more

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.740
about later is asset tracking.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.790
And I think this is a really
good example of why LPWAN

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.404
is going to help grow
the IoT because things

00:04:34.404 --> 00:04:36.070
like asset tracking
and fleet management

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.600
are not new IoT use cases.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.200
There's lots of companies
out there doing this today.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.270
But given the cost
of connectivity,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.660
whether it's keeping the
devices cellular connected

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.270
or having to recharge the
batteries all the time,

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.390
it's kept asset tracking only
to pretty high value assets.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:55.330
So companies still have a
hard time tracking everything

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.680
through the entire supply chain.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.310
So before we start talking
about new solutions

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.230
and how they can help
these industries,

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:08.440
let's look a little bit
about how things work today.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:14.010
You have three major options
for connectivity with IoT today.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.690
And I promise you probably
know about these if you're

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:18.507
here at this session
listening to this,

00:05:18.507 --> 00:05:20.590
but it will be important
for framing things later.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.798
You have personal area
networks, and these are things

00:05:22.798 --> 00:05:24.400
like Bluetooth, SIG, Thread.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.700
And they're great for
short range communication,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.070
and they already are
quite power efficient.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:33.790
There's lots of exciting things
happening in this space today.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.320
Then you have local
area networks.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.750
This is what a lot of IoT
devices choose to use,

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.550
things like Wi-Fi and ethernet.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.710
We have great existing
infrastructure for it.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.930
Almost everybody has
Wi-Fi in their house.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:45.850
It works most of the
time, unless you're here

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:49.100
at a conference like
this, then maybe it works.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.580
But even with advances
in costs, we still

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.340
have mediocre battery life on
Wi-Fi and ethernet devices.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:58.180
And finally, you have
traditional cellular options,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.450
which give great
range, great coverage,

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:04.420
and the networks are already
deployed in most places.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.400
But both the cost
of the modules,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.980
and the recurring costs of
paying for cellular data,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.230
can be quite expensive,
especially if you're

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.290
trying to connect
millions of assets

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.240
for whatever your project is.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.580
So now let's talk
about how LPWAN,

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.430
which is a technology that's
really a set of technologies,

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.730
which have really been driven
by the needs of IoT customers

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:29.200
and IoT use cases are
starting to change this.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.190
So LPWAN technologies can give
you much better battery life

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:35.620
than cellular and Wi-Fi.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.070
They can give you better range
than personal area networks

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.160
and can be cheaper than
traditional cellular,

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.400
but it does come at a cost of
much, much lower bandwidth.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.010
But for IoT applications,
this is actually

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.540
generally not a problem.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:52.150
Being able to sacrifice
bandwidth because you only

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:55.210
need to send a few bytes
of sensor data a day

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.580
actually makes a
lot of sense if you

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.890
can connect these
devices super cheaply,

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:04.240
have them run on a single
battery for years at a time,

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:11.330
while communicating to base
stations that are miles away.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:16.270
So let's see how LPWAN stacks
up to our existing connectivity

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.820
options in the few
key areas that we

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.640
talked about causing problems
for IoT connectivity today.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.530
So, in terms of
range and bandwidth,

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:28.162
you have personal area networks
that are sort of low on both,

00:07:28.162 --> 00:07:29.620
and then you have
cellular networks

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.500
that give great bandwidth
and great range today.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.130
LPWAN is somewhere down here
on the bottom, where you

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.750
have very, very low bandwidth.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:41.470
In some cases,
only bytes per day.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:45.400
But you actually have as good,
if not maybe a little bit

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.467
better, range with
some LPWAN technologies

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:48.550
than traditional cellular.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:52.660
Now let's look at cost.

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.040
You have pretty much the
existing infrastructure

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.920
and the PAM technologies are
very, very low cost today,

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.840
both in terms of module
and overall pricing.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.400
And then you have cellular,
which give great range.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.880
But not only are
modules more expensive,

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:13.420
but you have to pay a cellular
provider every single month.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.980
And even with new
IoT pricing plans,

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.980
it can be quite expensive
if you need to connect

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:20.420
large numbers of devices.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.410
And this is where
LPWAN is really

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:27.190
going to help companies bring
down the cost because you have,

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:30.190
again, just as good
a range as cellular,

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.010
but in many cases,
much lower costs.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.679
And that's driven by the
size of the payloads.

00:08:35.679 --> 00:08:38.289
But it's also driven by the
fact that, if you want to,

00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:41.230
you can deploy LPWAN
yourself and then you

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.210
don't have any network
provider that you're

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:46.840
paying every month, every year.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.270
Now let's talk about power
usage or, ultimately, how long

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:50.590
you're going to get on battery.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:52.960
And I think this is
where it really shines

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.720
through how great LPWAN is.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:59.410
You have your traditional PAN
technologies-- like Bluetooth,

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.740
Threads, IGBT, the
[INAUDIBLE] technologies--

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.230
down here where we have
very, very low power

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.720
usage but very, very low range.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.150
And then you have, in
the opposite corner,

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.100
traditional cellular.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.140
LPWAN, we're hoping,
is going to change this

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.960
because we can get almost
as good battery life

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.270
as we do with the personal
area network technologies

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:22.960
and almost as long a range
as with traditional cellular.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.050
So this set of technologies
is pretty exciting

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:31.930
going forward for helping
drive more adoption in IoT.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:33.640
So let's talk about
some of the options.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:36.280
And I'm going to let the other
guys get up here because they

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:37.270
are the experts.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:41.200
But you have Sigfox,
which is a single vendor

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.170
but multi-national network,
where you can go to them

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:47.750
and buy one radio that will
work in multiple places.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:51.130
You have LoRa, which is
driven by the LoRa Alliance

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:56.770
open network technology that
has network providers, which

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:59.730
support it in many places
in the world today.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:03.840
And then you have NB-IoT,
which is a standard from 3GPP,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.849
so the same standards group that
does the 3G and 4G standards.

00:10:07.849 --> 00:10:09.390
And typically these
are networks that

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:12.720
are deployed by traditional
telecom companies.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.540
Today we really want to
focus on talking about LoRa

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.080
and telling you more about that.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.590
So without further ado,
I'll have Vivek come up

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.330
and talk to you about it.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.454
VIVEK MOHAN: Thanks, Calum.

00:10:22.454 --> 00:10:24.430
[AUDIENCE APPLAUSE]

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:29.394
VIVEK MOHAN: So we're going
to touch on LoRa technology.

00:10:29.394 --> 00:10:31.060
And then Victor will
talk about how it's

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:34.340
used in an actual use case.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:35.847
So as we start,
you know, Semtech--

00:10:35.847 --> 00:10:38.180
for those of you who are not
familiar with the company--

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.000
we are a semiconductor company.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:45.180
We've been around for 60 years
designing analog mixed signal

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.570
IC's.

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.420
And about five years ago,
we saw customers trying

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.650
to solve a problem, right?

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:53.560
There's many use cases
that you can see up here

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:57.430
in the smart city segment, in
supply chain and logistics,

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:01.780
in smart buildings, where you
have these sensors that don't

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.940
have access to line power.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:08.410
They may be monitoring leaks
in a pipe in your basement,

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:11.860
they may be an elevator
shaft detecting vibration,

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:13.930
or it may be a water meter
or a gas meter that's

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:15.520
buried deep underground.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.460
And these don't send a lot of
data to the network, right?

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.610
So they may send, a few
times a day, small payloads.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.680
They need to have
really long range,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:27.820
and existing technologies don't
offer the power consumption

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.120
and the cost benefits.

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:31.810
So if you look at
Wi-Fi or Bluetooth,

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.490
you really need to add
in a lot of repeaters.

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:36.760
Or you need a mesh
network like Zigbee, where

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:38.270
it adds cost of infrastructure.

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:42.130
So if you look at the Capex
and OPEX, it doesn't work.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:43.930
So we brought a
lot of technology

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:47.140
to the market with the goal
of providing long range

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:49.540
connectivity at a
fraction of the cost

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:51.940
and fraction of power
consumption of existing

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.048
technologies.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.250
So what is LoRa, right?

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:02.840
There's fundamentally two
ways people send data out

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:04.280
of these sensor networks.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:07.400
You know, if you've attended
any of the IoT sessions

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:08.810
at this conference,
a lot of talk

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:12.590
is about edge computing
with the httpu announcement,

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:15.450
Cloud computing-- all
of this needs data.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:17.954
But the problem that
enterprises and businesses

00:12:17.954 --> 00:12:19.370
are trying to solve
is, how do you

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:22.890
extract data from these things
that could be located anywhere,

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:23.390
right?

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.520
That's a hard problem
to solve, and that's

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:27.750
where Semtech comes in.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:29.900
So if you look at
the figure here,

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:32.240
you can see that you
can either optimize

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.290
for frequency, spectrum.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:37.580
So if you're using licensed
spectrum, like NB-IoT or LTE,

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.350
traditionally the
entire network is

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:43.020
architected to be optimized
around spectrum efficiency,

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:43.520
right?

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:47.690
That's their main driver
because it's expensive.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:50.630
With LoRa, it operates
an unlicensed spectrum.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:53.540
And we use something called
spread spectrum, which

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:56.450
is beneficial because we
live in a very noisy RF

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:57.330
environment, right?

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:58.760
There's Wi-Fi,
there's Bluetooth,

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:01.160
there's all sorts of things
constantly transmitting

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:02.409
around us.

00:13:02.409 --> 00:13:03.950
So you need to have
a technology that

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:07.190
can [INAUDIBLE] robust in
the presence of interferers.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.490
And we'll spread spectrum,
which allows the receiver

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.100
to actually recover
the signal even if it's

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:18.690
below the noise level it's low
power and it's low complexity.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:22.100
So if you're to scale LPWAN
and IoT to the millions

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.380
and billions that
analysts are predicting,

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:29.570
the cost has to be extremely
low at the sensor side, which

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:32.060
means we don't want to add a
lot of processing complexity

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.640
or processing capability
all the way of these sensors

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.380
for these types of applications.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:40.070
So we focused on
building LoRa and LoRa

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:43.680
WAN on low power and
to keep the end nodes

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.830
and the hardware
costs really low.

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:48.540
It's bidirectional with
adaptive data rates.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.990
So if you can think about
it-- if you have a gateway,

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.440
and you have a sensor that's
maybe closer to the gateway,

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.876
it doesn't need to blast out
its packet at full power, right?

00:13:57.876 --> 00:13:59.000
You can back off the power.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:01.520
And all of that is network
controlled with something

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.669
called adaptive data rate.

00:14:03.669 --> 00:14:05.210
So you can trade
off of the data rate

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:10.580
for range and optimize power
consumption on the sensor side.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.830
It's tolerant to moving objects.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:14.900
So for asset management,
which, you know,

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.090
Victor show you an
example of how LoRa WAN is

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.640
used in asset tracking.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:21.120
You can still use this.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:23.060
I mentioned robust interference.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:26.210
And one of the key features
related to asset management

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:29.120
is GPS-free geolocation.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.910
So you can do this if you
have an application or a use

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:35.030
case that requires
anywhere from maybe 50

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.700
meters in a rural
environment to 150

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:39.470
meters in an urban environment.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:42.452
We're not talking about
submeter accuracy, right?

00:14:42.452 --> 00:14:43.910
But there's plenty
of applications.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:47.810
You may need to do geofencing
or see if a car in your fleet

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:51.900
has left a certain
area, things like that.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:55.670
So there's many use cases
where GPS, as we all know,

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:57.770
is extremely power hungry,
and it obviously adds

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:59.580
cost to the sensor as well.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:03.430
So we figured out a way using
time difference of arrival

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:06.830
to know where the sensor is
without adding additional cost

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:08.000
on the sensor itself.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:16.570
So it provides good location
for outdoor applications

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:18.170
without battery
impact, in the sense

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:21.320
that there is no GPS trying
to acquire satellites

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.534
in a position fixed constantly.

00:15:23.534 --> 00:15:24.950
It's bidirectional,
so if you want

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.080
to do firmware
updates over the air.

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.720
Now, in these applications,
because the sensors

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:34.380
may be in places that are
very difficult to access,

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.160
but they still need to
last five 10, 15, even 20

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.430
years in some
cases in the field,

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:42.260
over-the-air updates
are important.

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:44.810
You can have acknowledgments
come back from the network

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.870
indicating that the
network's received a packet.

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:49.580
And also for key
exchange, right?

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:52.110
You can activate
sensors over the air,

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.720
so you need bi-directional
communication.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:58.660
LoRa WAN is driven
by the LoRa Alliance.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.850
It's an industrial lines
of over 500 companies,

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:04.940
and LoRa WAN is the
MAC Layer protocol.

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:08.220
And the aim of this is to
standardize and open-source

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.150
the MAC Layer protocol so
that you have interoperability

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:13.640
amongst all these sensors,
and we can kind of

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:16.310
define some standards around
it, which is a real way

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.290
to scale all of these networks.

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:20.540
It's got global
support, and it also

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.720
supports roaming
across networks.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:25.846
And security is
really important.

00:16:25.846 --> 00:16:28.220
You know, these end nodes,
they may have a secure element

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:30.950
in some cases to store the keys.

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:34.340
So security and provisioning
is also thought of all the way

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:38.040
from the sensor to the Cloud
and different layers in between.

00:16:41.402 --> 00:16:43.360
So what does the architecture
look like, right?

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.230
You've got a bunch of
sensors transmitting data,

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:50.220
and most of these are
uplinked to the gateways.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:53.190
The way this is architected--
it's a start apology.

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.520
And any gateway within
range of a sensor

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:59.640
will be able to receive
the packet, right?

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.010
So typically, in
an installation,

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:06.930
you have more than one
gateway that a sensor can hit.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:10.060
So the sensors send
packets up to the gateway.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.810
The gateways are pretty
simple packet forwarders.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.750
There's not a huge amount of
processing complexity, again,

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:19.020
with the aim of offloading
all of that processing

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:21.390
to places where you have
the capability to do that

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:23.079
and the resources to do that.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:25.859
So we want to keep the
cost of these gateways--

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.910
not only of the sensors, but
also the gateways-- really low,

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:30.900
right?

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.660
And then you've got the network
server and the application

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.950
server, which could
reside in the Cloud

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:38.580
or maybe on premise
in some applications

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:40.429
if you have other concerns.

00:17:40.429 --> 00:17:41.970
So one thing that's
important to note

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:46.910
is, you know, Calum mentioned
Sigfox and NB-IoT and LoRa's

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:47.760
options, right?

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:50.220
And when we think about
wide area networks,

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:54.000
we typically think of the
mobile network operators.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.670
In the case of LoRa, you can
actually build private networks

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:59.790
as well because the cost
of the infrastructure

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.800
and the simplicity of the
protocol allow you to do that.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:05.280
So if you've got a
specific area to cover,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:08.440
and you want to do that and you
can afford the infrastructure,

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.926
you can go out and do that and
still be LoRa WAN compliant

00:18:10.926 --> 00:18:12.300
and build your
network with that.

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.490
So there's multiple options
in terms of deployment

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:16.980
strategies-- it can be
private, it can be public,

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:18.540
it can be a hybrid mix of both.

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:25.810
So I talked about
the LoRa Alliance.

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:28.960
I mentioned that it's the
industrial lines that's

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:30.940
defining the LoRa WAN standard.

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:33.140
It's an openly
available specification,

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:36.810
so you can access it from
the LoRa Alliance website.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:40.240
It's a large ecosystem, from
Silicon providers like Semtech,

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:43.120
to solutions, and
everything in between.

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:45.170
It's got a global
certification program

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:49.270
to ensure a certain minimum
level of quality of sensors,

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:51.940
and it defines
end-to-end security.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:54.040
So one thing to
point out here is

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:56.560
LoRa WAN is really
focused on the Mac, right?

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:59.260
The physical layer--
the radio itself--

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.930
is LoRa, and that's a
modulation-- a spread spectrum

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:03.060
modulation.

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:06.140
The MAC Layer is where
you have multiple options.

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.040
So you've got three
different classes

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:13.940
in LoRa WAN, which trade
latency for power consumption.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:19.150
So, in class A, if you have a
sensor that will send data only

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:23.680
when it needs to, and there's
no network initiated traffic,

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:25.180
then that's your
Class A mode, which

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:29.020
is optimized for the lowest,
lowest power consumption.

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:31.570
But the latency's higher because
your network can actually

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:34.810
initiate a communication
with the sensor.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:37.450
Then you've got
Class B, which is

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:41.350
similar to a beaconing system,
where the network sends out

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:42.970
periodic beacons.

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:45.010
And the sensors listen
to that and they wake up

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:47.410
at those intervals kind of
similar to Wi-Fi beacons

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:50.800
if you want to think about
it that way, which, you know,

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.230
has maybe a little bit higher
power consumption than class

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:55.990
A mode, but still is a good
balance between latency

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:57.790
and power consumption.

00:19:57.790 --> 00:20:00.760
And then you've got Class
C, where the sensors are

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:03.310
constantly receiving, right?

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.680
This could be an application,
like a street light.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:07.240
We've got a lot of
land deployments

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:09.960
that are going into street
lights in smart cities,

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.220
and the street lights
always have line power,

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:14.800
so power consumption
is not a huge concern

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.000
for these streetlights.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:17.500
So they're always receiving.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:19.660
The other way Class
C is used is if you

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:22.540
want to do firmware updates
over the air, right?

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:25.720
We all know that LoRa WAN is
a low bandwidth, low data rate

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:26.620
system.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:30.680
So updating firmware
could take a few minutes,

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:34.282
maybe half an hour, depending on
how much data you want to send.

00:20:34.282 --> 00:20:35.740
So one of the ways
it's implemented

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:38.950
is you could actually switch
your sensor to Class C mode,

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:41.950
get your updates done quickly,
and then switch it back

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:43.270
to Class A, right?

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:45.670
So there's different ways
of implementing it and still

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:47.653
optimizing for
power consumption.

00:20:51.127 --> 00:20:52.710
So if you want
additional information,

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:55.220
Semtech hosts something
called a LoRa community.

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:59.210
It's a website where we
list products, services,

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:01.700
from ecosystem partners.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:03.260
You can collaborate
with others that

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:05.600
have adopted LoRa technology.

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:07.250
There's about 50
million sensors today

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:09.833
that are actually deployed, so
the technology itself, I think,

00:21:09.833 --> 00:21:11.720
is quite proven.

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:13.970
You can get support from
our technical experts

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:17.120
in the community, and get
a lot of good resources

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:19.370
in terms of application
notes and use cases.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:24.430
And, finally, the
LoRa WAN Academy--

00:21:24.430 --> 00:21:26.350
so one interesting
thing we started seeing

00:21:26.350 --> 00:21:29.950
was a lot of
university professors

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:34.810
were interested in IoT courses
and experimenting with IoT

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:37.480
and teaching the next
generation of engineers.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:40.960
So together with partners, we've
created a LoRa WAN Academy,

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:44.770
which essentially gives
universities a LoRa

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:47.470
gateway and a few sensors.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:51.890
And it's got a full
curriculum that's all online.

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:56.170
So it's provided as courses
in different universities.

00:21:56.170 --> 00:21:58.420
We've got about 10 universities
signed up right now.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:00.850
And it's a full
package that professors

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:03.190
can use to teach courses,
they can use it for research

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:06.370
into the LPWAN space,
and prepare, really,

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:09.290
everybody for real
world IoT, right?

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:11.290
So for additional information
on that, you know,

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:16.200
you can check out the
LoRa WAN Academy website.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:19.730
With that said, I'm going
to hand it back to Calum.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:21.650
